# The corpus every implementation of an `answer` reads.
#
# `ask` is answered by `answer`, the third of the three pairs. Three client
# libraries take one apart, and they have to arrive at the same structure from
# the same text or the same answer means three things.
#
# Each case is two lines. The first is what arrived:
#
#   answer <the arguments after the verb>
#
# The second is what it has to come out as, written back from the structure:
#
#   want <the same thing, in canonical spelling>
#   bad          (the text must be refused)
#
# `want` is the structure rendered again, so a part dropped on the way in is a
# part missing on the way out.
#
# The ENVELOPE is what is agreed here: which question is being answered, whether
# this is the last piece, and whether it is a refusal. The payload is the
# question's own and no implementation reads it -- whoever asked knows what the
# answer to that question looks like -- so what the cases check about it is that
# it survives untouched and in order.
#
# See wire/answer.go.

# --- the envelope, at its smallest ---
answer complete
want complete

# A bare terminator is NOT read as the question it is answering. `result` puts
# its query id bare and gets away with it because a query id is a number; a
# correlation key is a name, so this would take `complete` for one -- and an
# answer ending a question nobody asked is dropped, leaving the real asker
# waiting for good.
answer to=q1 complete
want to=q1 complete

# --- the question's own arguments, kept in order and untouched ---
answer to=q1 id=42 how=altered fields={ kind "Archive" }
want to=q1 id=42 how=altered fields={ kind "Archive" }

answer to=q1 id=42 how=replaced record={ name "a.txt"; size 1024 }
want to=q1 id=42 how=replaced record={ name "a.txt"; size 1024 }

# A removal carries neither: the record is gone, so there is nothing of it to
# state, and members left on one would be written out as though in force.
answer to=q1 id=9 how=removed
want to=q1 id=9 how=removed

# A flag among the payload is a flag, not an envelope argument.
answer to=q1 id=3 how=added clashed
want to=q1 id=3 how=added clashed

# --- the payload and the terminator on one statement ---
answer to=q1 count=2 complete
want to=q1 count=2 complete

# --- an unkeyed ask is answered without a correlation ---
answer count=0 complete
want count=0 complete

# --- a refusal is still an answer, and it completes ---
#
# Written back with the terminator before the reason, which is the canonical
# order whichever order arrived: the envelope reads correlation, then payload,
# then end, then why. What is compared is the structure.
answer to=q1 error="no question called amendments" complete
want to=q1 complete error="no question called amendments"

# --- the envelope's names are not the question's to use ---
answer to=1
bad
answer to="q1"
bad
answer complete="yes"
bad
answer error=nothing
bad
